Runbook: Floor 6 Legal Slow/Failed Windows Logon (Win11 + Intune)
Version: 1.0
Date: 2026-08-14
Use case: Engineer takes over cold during active user-impacting login incident.

1. Prerequisites

Access rights required before starting
- Intune admin portal access with permission to view and edit device configuration/profile assignments. [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED]
- Entra ID sign-in log reader access (or equivalent identity log access). [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED]
- Local admin rights on at least one affected endpoint for event log review and profile checks. [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED]
- Service desk ticket system access to record timeline and actions.
- Change record access for the recent Win11/Intune rollout affecting Floor 6.

Tools required
- Intune admin center.
- Entra ID (or equivalent identity log portal).
- Remote support tool approved by DWP.
- PowerShell 5.1 on target endpoint.
- Event Viewer on target endpoint.

Systems and data required
- One confirmed affected host name.
- One confirmed affected user ID.
- One control user on Floor 6 who is currently not impacted (if available).
- Incident ticket ID.
- Latest change ticket ID tied to Win11/Intune baseline for Floor 6.

2. Procedure

Step 1
Action: Open the active incident ticket and set status to "In Progress - Login Outage Triage".
Expected result: Incident ownership is visible and timestamped.

Step 2
Action: Record one affected user ID and one affected host name in the incident ticket.
Expected result: The ticket has a fixed test pair for repeatable validation.

Step 3
Action: Connect to the affected host using the approved remote support tool.
Expected result: You have an active support session on the affected endpoint.

Step 4
Action: Run `dsregcmd /status` on the affected host and capture output in the ticket. [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED if restricted]
Expected result: Device join and token state are documented for the impacted endpoint.

Step 5
Action: Open Event Viewer and export the last 2 hours of Security log events with IDs 4624, 4625, 4771, and 4776. [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED]
Expected result: Authentication success/failure evidence is available for correlation.

Step 6
Action: Open Event Viewer and export the last 2 hours of `Microsoft-Windows-User Profiles Service/Operational`. [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED]
Expected result: Profile load and profile error events are captured.

Step 7
Action: In Intune, open the affected device record and run a manual "Sync" action. [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED]
Expected result: A fresh policy check-in is queued for the affected device.

Step 8
Action: In Intune, open the recent sign-in/profile-related policy assigned to Floor 6 and capture the current assignment state. [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED]
Expected result: Current targeted scope is recorded before any containment change.

Step 9
Action: Pause or unassign the suspected sign-in/profile policy from Floor 6 device group only. [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED]
Expected result: New policy application to Floor 6 is stopped for the suspected setting.

Step 10
Action: Trigger a second manual "Sync" on the same affected device in Intune. [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED]
Expected result: The device requests updated effective policy state after containment.

Step 11
Action: Ask the affected user to sign in again on the same host.
Expected result: User reaches desktop without abnormal delay.

Step 12
Action: Measure and record time from credential submit to desktop readiness.
Expected result: A measurable post-fix login duration is captured.

Step 13
Action: Repeat sign-in test with one additional Floor 6 user on one additional host.
Expected result: Secondary validation confirms issue is not isolated to a single user/device.

Step 14
Action: Check Intune device status for both tested hosts and confirm successful sync completion. [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED]
Expected result: Both hosts show current policy check-in status after containment.

Step 15
Action: Update incident ticket with "Service Restored" and include evidence references (logs, sync times, login test times).
Expected result: Closure evidence is auditable and complete.

Step 16
Action: Create a follow-up problem record to identify the exact misconfigured policy setting/value.
Expected result: Root-cause deep dive is tracked separately after service restoration.

3. Verification

Complete all checks before closure
- Check 1: Affected user can log in to the original affected host.
- Check 2: Login duration is back to expected baseline for the floor (use local baseline or pre-incident baseline if available).
- Check 3: A second user on a second Floor 6 host can log in successfully.
- Check 4: No new repeating authentication/profile failure pattern appears in the next 30 minutes of Security and User Profile logs. [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED]
- Check 5: Intune sync status for tested hosts shows successful policy refresh. [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED]

Closure rule
- Close as "Resolved" only after Checks 1 to 5 pass.

4. Rollback (immediately actionable)

Use this section if login behavior worsens after Step 9 containment.

Rollback Step 1
Action: Re-assign the paused/unassigned policy back to its exact pre-change group scope using the captured assignment state from Step 8. [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED]
Expected result: Original policy targeting is restored exactly.

Rollback Step 2
Action: Trigger Intune manual "Sync" on each test host used in Steps 11 and 13. [ELEVATED PERMISSIONS REQUIRED]
Expected result: Restored policy state is requested by test hosts.

Rollback Step 3
Action: Reboot each test host one time.
Expected result: Hosts restart with restored policy application path.

Rollback Step 4
Action: Re-run sign-in test with the same affected user on the original host.
Expected result: Post-rollback login behavior is confirmed and documented.

Rollback Step 5
Action: Escalate to L2 endpoint engineering with exported logs and the exact policy assignment diff.
Expected result: Higher-tier team has complete evidence for immediate takeover.

5. Notes

Edge cases
- If Security log access is denied, collect only User Profile and System logs first, then request temporary elevated support access.
- If Intune sync does not complete within expected window, wait for portal status refresh interval and re-check before changing additional policies.
- If only one user remains affected after service restoration, treat as user/profile-specific sub-incident, not floor-wide recurrence.

Warnings
- Do not delete user profiles during active business hours unless L2 explicitly approves and user impact is accepted.
- Do not apply broad policy rollback outside Floor 6 scope during initial containment.
- Do not close incident based on one successful login test alone.

Related incidents
- Copilot matter exposure concern is a separate security/access incident and must not be merged into this login runbook.
- Desktop shortcut loss after document-management rollout is a separate endpoint configuration incident and should follow its own runbook.